## Why This Is Asked
Tests a realistic real-time WebSocket scenario and beginner-friendly granularity (handshake, message latency, backpressure) that is frequently encountered in consumer apps and fintech-grade feeds.

## Key Concepts
- WebSocket handshake latency and maintenance cost
- Latency vs throughput trade-offs
- Backpressure signals and broker queues
- Ramp-up strategy and warm-up
- Basic observability: latency percentiles, error rate

## Code Example
```javascript
import ws from 'k6/ws';
import { check } from 'k6';
export default function () {
  const url = __ENV.WS_URL || 'wss://example.com/feed';
  const res = ws.connect(url, {}, function (socket) {
    socket.on('open', function () { socket.send(JSON.stringify({type:'ping'})); });
    socket.on('message', function (data) { /* parse */ });
  });
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adjust the plan for mobile networks vs desktop?
- What instrumentation would you add to quantify backpressure and queue depth?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests edge caching behavior, tail latency, and backpressure under regional surge—a practical beginner scenario for performance testing.

## Key Concepts
- Edge caching and cache warming
- Tail latency (p95/p99)
- API gateway backpressure and circuit breakers
- Regional autoscaling and cost/latency trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
import http from 'k6/http';
import { sleep } from 'k6';
export let options = {
  scenarios: {
    regionalSurge: { executor: 'ramping-vus', startVUs: 0, stages: [ { duration: '2m', target: 50 }, { duration: '8m', target: 200 } ] }
  }
};
export default function () {
  http.get('https://region-A.example.com/pricing');
  http.post('https://region-A.example.com/book', JSON.stringify({}), { headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' } });
  sleep(0.5);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would network jitter influence your test results and how would you compensate for it?
- What signals would you use to decide if the edge cache warming was successful?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a beginner’s ability to plan end-to-end perf testing across a real-time microservices stack, emphasizing practical isolation and measurable outcomes.

## Key Concepts
- End-to-end latency distribution (p95/p99)
- Realistic load profiles (staged ramp, concurrency targets)
- Component isolation via feature flags
- Metrics across gateway, auth, cache, and data-join layers

## Code Example
```javascript
import http from 'k6/http';
import { sleep, group } from 'k6';
export let options = { stages: [ { duration: '5m', target: 100 }, { duration: '20m', target: 800 }, { duration: '5m', target: 50 } ] };
export default function() {
  group('ride-forecast', () => {
    http.get('https://api.example.com/forecast');
  });
  sleep(0.5);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you detect cache stampede and mitigate it?
- If DNS or network jitter caused outliers, what adjustments would you make to the test and metrics?

## Why This Is Asked
A real-time streaming pipeline with backpressure and external dependencies tests more than single endpoints; ensures autoscaling correctness and end-to-end latency budgets.

## Key Concepts
- End-to-end latency in streaming pipelines
- Backpressure and circuit breakers
- Workload modeling for bursts vs steady-state
- Autoscaling behavior and metrics
- Bottleneck localization across Kafka, enrichment, external API, DB

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple Kafka load generator (pseudo) using kafkajs
const { Kafka } = require('kafkajs');
const kafka = new Kafka({ clientId: 'load', brokers: ['kafka:9092'] });
const producer = kafka.producer();
async function run(n) {
  await producer.connect();
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    await producer.send({ topic: 'fraud', messages: [{ value: JSON.stringify({ id: i, amount: Math.random() * 1000 }) }] });
  }
  await producer.disconnect();
}
run(1000000).catch(console.error);
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you quantify backpressure impact on downstream services?
- How would you adapt the test when external API SLA degrades?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to design end-to-end performance tests spanning multiple subsystems with realistic data flows, backpressure, and failure modes; probes tail latency budgets during promos and how caching, storage, and batch layers interact under stress.

## Key Concepts

- Tail latency
- End-to-end tracing across Redis, Cassandra, embeddings, Spark
- Workload modeling: read/write mix, skew, burstiness
- Backpressure mechanisms: queues, circuit breakers, autoscaling
- Observability: metrics, logs, distributed tracing
- Failure modes: eviction, timeouts, model cold-start

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-load test skeleton (k6)
import http from 'k6/http';
import { check, sleep } from 'k6';
export default function () {
  http.get('https://svc.example/recommend?user_id=' + Math.floor(Math.random()*100000));
  sleep(0.05);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate that cache warmth is not masking data skew?
- What monitoring dashboards and alerting thresholds would you set for p99 across components?
